      "answer": "Principal differential diagnoses include strychnine poisoning, hypocalcemic tetany, drug-induced dystonia, CNS infections (e.g., meningitis, encephalitis), stiff-person syndrome, and seizure disorders. Severe sepsis may mimic some autonomic features but does not explain the stimulus-triggered spasms or trismus.",
      "rationale": "Strychnine poisoning closely mimics tetanus with similar spasms and opisthotonus. Hypocalcemia causes tetany but lacks trismus and autonomic features. Dystonic reactions are usually acute and drug-related. CNS infections may cause rigidity but typically involve altered mental status. Stiff-person syndrome is chronic and progressive. Seizures lack the sustained, stimulus-triggered nature of tetanus spasms.",
